At extrapolation of the obtained experimental data to a commercial scale furnace, certain decrease of sulphur content should be expected as compared to the pilot test results. Still, in any case, decrease of sulphur content in the calcine would not exceed 0.3wt%. 

Chemical analyses revealed that residual sulphur contained in the calcine is, on the average, presented by sulphide sulphur (90%) and sulphate sulphur (10%). Therefore, the fact that some portion of sulphide minerals cannot be oxidised even at the protracted fluidised bed’ residence time is the reason preventing from deep sulphur removal. SEM and EPMA studies of the calcine samples showed that sulphide mineral particles were covered with a dense layer of copper ferrites formed on the calcine’ surface (see Fig. 10). 



