Depending on the smelting, converting and dust treatment technologies in place at a particular smelter, a small fraction of the input arsenic will be retained by the anode copper.  If we assume that a smelter has 10,000 tons per year of arsenic input and that 10% (1,000 tons per annum) reports to the blister copper, based on the above, the reagent cost for this example would be on the order of US$1.5M per year. As discussed by Hache et al. [23] the As penalty charged by a copper smelter could be approximately $3,000/tonne of arsenic. For the above case, revenues from the arsenic penalty may reach US$30 M; hence the cost of sulphate fluxing in the anode furnace (1.5M$) may be considered quite reasonable. 

Another important aspect is the recycling/disposal of this high arsenic/low copper salt.  Even though the salt has no significant copper solubility, it is in fact difficult to fully separate the metallic copper, the copper rich slag and the salt. At least three options could be considered to handle this salt: 

- Return the salt in the molten or solid state to the smelting vessel; 

- Solidify and mill the salt for feed it back to the smelting unit, for example as a coolant; 

- Develop alternative technologies for disposable of the arsenic as a stable phase for hazardous dump sites such as ferric arsenate. 

These aspects need careful consideration. 

### **CONCLUSIONS** 

This paper presented a review of the available published information on the fire refining of copper using molten sulphate fluxes. The study showed that this technique is capable of safely and efficiently removing arsenic from blister copper. In addition, the buffering action of the molten slag with copper provides for an effective means of retaining a certain level of residual As in anode copper in order to meet electrorefinery arsenic requirements. The advantages and limitations of the technique were discussed in the paper. 

The main benefits of this technology were outlined in the paper and can be summarized as follows: 

- i. Very effective for arsenic removal from copper and can handle a small amount of antimony; the technique can effectively replace soda fluxing; 

- ii. The fluxing step can be adjusted so as to leave an appropriate level of arsenic in anode  copper suitable for the electro-refining stage (the salt acts as an arsenic buffer); 

- iii. Effectively refines blister copper for arsenic while considerably reducing arsenic volatilization from the anode furnace compared to soda fluxing;

