At commissioning in May 1999, the SMC vessel was equipped with 13 shrouded injectors installed radially with a 2.5 cm O.D. (1.0 in.) and designed for a flow rate of 752 Nm<sup>3</sup> /h/injector. The injectors were designed with a more conservative cooling factor of 50-55% rather than the lower value of 30-35% used during the last demonstration test. The nominal flow rates for the oxygen enriched air and nitrogen streams were initially 8 240 Nm<sup>3</sup> /h and 1 537 Nm<sup>3</sup> /h, respectively, while their nominal pressures were 520 kPag and 620 kPag (75 psig and 90 psig), respectively. The gas supply system was capable of providing an oxygen enriched air pressure of up to 690 kPag (100 psig) and a nitrogen pressure of up to 1 030 kPag (150 psig). Over the following months and years, as all stakeholders in the converter aisle were becoming sufficiently comfortable with the new SMC vessel operation, modifications were made to the injectors to continuously increase the vessel intensity [23-25]. Some of the key developments were as follows: 

- August 1999 – Oxygen enrichment level increased from 30% to 40%. Number of injectors reduced from 13 to 9. New, larger injector design with a 3.2 cm O.D. (1.25 in.) and 1 072 Nm<sup>3</sup> /h/injector flow rate capacity. Annular space dimension unchanged to maintain cooling factor of 50-55%; 

- January 2000 – Annular gap size reduced to lower cooling factor down to the 3035%. Overall oxygen enrichment maintained at 40%; 

- May 2002 – Rotation of injector mounting assembly by 34 degrees requiring a slightly longer injector and allowing a 15 cm (6 in.) increase of injector submergence, an increase in bath stirring, and a further shift away from refractory bricks (goal to lower pear-shaped refractory wear immediately above the injectors – see Figure 21); 

- May 2002 – Reduction of the number of active injectors down to 6, with lowering of total blast rate down to about 6 450 Nm<sup>3</sup> /h and increase in oxygen enrichment to 43%; 

- 2005 – Replacement of hood and back flap resulting in large reduction of fugitive emissions from the vessel; 

- June 2006 – Replacement of Garr gun with central feeding chute and use of a modified shrouded injector (inner pipe replaced by a blank) to inject nitrogen only and provide stirring of the bath under the chute. Cold charge feeding increased by of 45%. 

